 8823: 
 8824:                   Because the chip does not provide an serr_l signal pin, external logic
 8825:                   must report serr_l assertions on one of the chip's irq<2:0> interrupt
 8826:                   signal pins.
 8827: 
 8828:             Configuration cycles use address stepping, which drives the address and
 8829:             command for two consecutive cycles. The frame_l signal is asserted during the
 8830:             second cycle.
 8831: 
 8832: 6.2.6 Sparse Memory Space
 8833: 
 8834:             The sparse memory space is the region in which maskable, CPU-initiated PCI
 8835:             transactions occur. This region uses the low bits of the CPU address to indicate
 8836:             transfer size and uses the IOC_HAE register to extend the shifted address to
 8837:             cover the 32-bit PCI address space.
 8838: 
 8839: 6.2.6.1 Generating PCI Addresses for Sparse Space
 8840:             CPU physical address bits <31:7> are used for PCI address bits <26:2>. To
 8841:             support various transfer size (byte enable) combinations in sparse space, the
 8842:             low-order bits of the CPU physical addresses that reference PCI address spaces
 8843:             are encoded as follows:
 8844: 
 8845:             � Physical address bits <6:3> generate the PCI byte enable c_be_l<3:0>
 8846:                   signals. Byte enables generated by combinations of physical address bits
 8847:                   <6:3>, not shown in Table 6-3, are unspecified.
 8848: 
 8849:                                                                                                                 I/O Controller 6-7
 8850:     � Physical address bits <4:3> also specify transfer size.
 8851: 
 8852:     � Physical address bits <2:0> must be zero for transfers in sparse space.
 8853: 
 8854:     Table 6-3 shows how the low-order physical address bits are used to indicate
 8855:     transfer size.
 8856: 
 8857: Table 6-3 Byte Enable Encoding for CPU-Initiated Transfers in Sparse Space
 8858: 
 8859:  CPU CPU            BytePECnIables3          PCI       PCI      Transfer
 8860: a<6:5> a<4:3> Size                   Memory Cycles  I/O Cycles    Type
 9190:                   used.
 9191: 
 9192:             � PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from physical address bits a<6:5>
 9193:                   during CPU-initiated PCI transfers to I/O space.
 9194: 
 9195:             � An I/O read or write command (Table 6-2) is issued on the c_be_l<3:0>
 9196:                   pins coincident with a read or write operation to this region.
 9197: 
 9198:             The I/O region supports all masked and unmasked transfers that are supported
 9199:             in sparse space.
 9200: 
 9201: 6.2.9 Configuration Cycles
 9202: 
 9203:             The PCI architecture specifies a configuration address space for defining
 9204:             configuration registers that are used to initialize and configure devices.
 9205:             The IOC supports transfers to the configuration address space to access
 9206:             the configuration registers. This region supports the same access types and
 9207:             masked address generation as sparse space (Section 6.2.6), with the following
 9208:             exceptions:
 9209: 
 9210:             � Configuration space is accessed with CPU address bits a<33:32> = 01 and
 9211:                   a<31:29> = 111.
 9212: 
 9213:             � PCI address bits ad<31:27> = 00000 and the IOC_HAE register is not
 9214:                   used.
 9215: 
 9216:             � PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type
 9217:                   register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0> during CPU-initiated PCI
 9218:                   transfers to configuration space.
 9219: 
 9220:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-15
 9221:             � A configuration space read or write command (Table 6-2) is issued on the
 9222:                   c_be_l<3:0> pins coincident with a read or write operation to this region.
 9223: 
 9224:             During configuration cycles, addresses are restricted both by the encoding
 9225:             requirements of PCI configuration cycles (as described in the PCI Local Bus
 9226:             Specification, Revision 2.0) and by the system-dependent implementation of
 9227:             PCI initialization device select (IDSEL) encoding. Configuration cycles use one
 9228:             cycle of address stepping; that is, the address and command are driven for two
 9229:             consecutive cycles.
 9230: 
 9231: 6.2.10 Special Cycles and Interrupt Acknowledge Cycles
 9232: 
 9233:             The PCI special cycle command implements a simple broadcast mech-
 9234:             anism that allows the broadcast of processor status information and
 9235:             sideband signaling. The command is not addressed to a specific device,
 9236:             and the receivers determine whether the message applies to them.
 9237:             Section 6.3.11 describes two supported sideband signals. Also see the
 9238:             PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0 for more information.
 9239: 
 9240: 6.2.10.1 Special Cycle
 9462: 
 9463:             � Logs the PCI command code of the CPU-initiated transfer in which the
 9464:                   error was detected into the CMD field of the IOC_STAT0 register. The
 9465:                   CMD field indicates the type of PCI cycle in which the error occurred.
 9466: 
 9467:             � Logs the starting address of the CPU-initiated PCI transfer into the ADDR
 9468:                   field of the IOC status 1 register (IOC_STAT1, Section 6.4.4).
 9469: 
 9470:             The ERR bit and the logged error information are frozen until the ERR bit is
 9471:             cleared by writing a one to it.
 9472: 
 9473:             If the ERR bit was set, the IOC sets the LOST bit in the IOC_STAT0 register.
 9474:             The LOST bit remains set until it is cleared by writing a one to it. The CODE
 9475:             and CMD error information is logged only for the error that caused the ERR
 9476:             bit to be set--such information for the second error is lost.
 9477: 
 9478:             See the PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0 for more information about
 9479:             the target-abort termination.
 9480: 
 9481: 6.2.18 PCI Master Abort
 9482: 
 9483:             The IOC terminates a CPU-initiated PCI transaction if no PCI device responds
 9484:             by asserting devsel_l within five PCI clock cycles after the IOC asserts the
 9485:             frame_l signal. The IOC will not retry this transfer on the PCI. If the CPU-
 9486:             initiated access was a read operation, the IOC returns arbitrary data with
 9487:             error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception. The IOC also
 9488:             reports the error to the CPU with an internal interrupt, and logs the error
 9489:             condition.
 9490: 
 9491:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-21
 9492:             The IOC does the following:
 9493: 
 9494:             � Sets the ERR bit (if it is not set) in the IOC status 0 register (IOC_STAT0,
 9495:                   Section 6.4.3).
 9496: 
 9497:             � Sets the error CODE field to 1 (no device) in the IOC_STAT0 register,
 9498:                   except for CPU-initiated PCI special cycles.
 9499: 
 9500:             � Logs the PCI command code of the CPU-initiated transfer in which the
 9501:                   error was detected into the CMD field of the IOC_STAT0 register. The
 9502:                   command code indicates the type of PCI cycle in which the error occurred.
 9503: 
 9504:             � Logs the starting address of the CPU-initiated PCI transfer into the ADDR
 9505:                   field of the IOC status 1 register (IOC_STAT1, Section 6.4.4).
 9506: 
 9507:             The ERR bit and the logged error information are frozen until the ERR bit is
 9508:             cleared by writing a one to it.
 9509: 
 9510:             If the ERR bit was set, the IOC sets the LOST bit in the IOC_STAT0 register.
 9511:             The LOST bit remains set until it is cleared by writing a one to it. The CODE
 9512:             and CMD error information is logged only for the error that caused the ERR
 9513:             bit to be set--such information for the second error is lost.
 9514: 
 9515: 6.3 Peripheral-Initiated PCI Cycles
 9516: 
 9517:             The IOC is a PCI target when it responds to cycles initiated by a PCI
 9518:             peripheral device or external DMA controller operating as a bus master. The
 9519:             IOC treats PCI-initiated masked read operations as unmasked read operations.
 9520:             Arbitrary byte-enable combinations for PCI-initiated write transfers are
 9521:             implemented by read-modify-write operations in the memory controller. PCI-
 9522:             initiated transfers can target pages of memory that are mapped in cacheable
 9523:             or noncacheable address spaces. The chip ensures that memory coherency is
 9524:             maintained for all PCI-initiated transfers to cacheable memory.
