# GXemul build notes (build/)

Built & verified 2026-06-27 (clean `make -j`, **0 warnings / 0 errors**). This
`build/` tree is the **fixed `est/` source**, compiled to a working `gxemul` binary.

## Toolchain
- **WSL Gentoo** (imported under `user00a` from `User00`'s image at
  `C:\WSL\Gentoo\ext4.vhdx`), reachable as `wsl -d Gentoo`.
- **gcc 15.2.1** (Gentoo), **GNU Make 4.4.1**, binutils ld 2.46, glibc, libX11.

## Build commands
```sh
cd /mnt/c/gxemul-0.7.0/build
sh ./configure          # auto-adds -fgnu89-inline (correction #9) �?" no manual CFLAGS
make -j"$(nproc)"       # parallel-safe since correction #61
```
Produces `./gxemul` �?" a dynamically-linked **Linux x86-64 ELF** (run it inside
WSL; it is not a native Windows .exe).

> **Parallel builds:** a stock `make -j` used to fail *intermittently* while
> generating `src/devices/autodev.c` �?" the recursive-make chain
> (`all`�+'`objs`�+'`autodev.o`) could run the non-atomic generator concurrently,
> corrupting a random `device_register`/`pci_register` line (`expected ';' before
> ')'` at a moving line number). Correction #61 makes `makeautodev.sh` write a
> PID-unique temp file and atomically `mv` it into place, so `-j` is now reliable.

## The one required fix: `-fgnu89-inline`
A stock `./configure && make` compiles all 265 objects but **fails at link** with:
```
multiple definition of `__cmsg_nxthdr' / `recv' / `recvfrom' / `inet_ntop' / `inet_pton'
```
Root cause: this is 2021 code written for the GNU89 `extern inline` semantics.
Modern glibc + GCC 10+ (and Gentoo enabling `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` by default) turn
glibc's `extern __inline` socket wrappers into an external definition in *every*
translation unit that includes `<sys/socket.h>`/`<arpa/inet.h>` (pulled in widely
via the common headers), so the linker sees dozens of duplicate definitions.

`-fgnu89-inline` restores the original inline semantics (an `extern inline`
function emits no external symbol), eliminating all the duplicates. It is the
standard, low-risk fix for this exact symbol set and changes no behavior.

> **Now applied (correction #9):** `configure` auto-appends `-fgnu89-inline` on
> glibc hosts after a feature test, so a plain `sh ./configure && make` links
> cleanly with no manual `CFLAGS`. (Correction #11 also removes the underlying
> `#define __attribute__(x)` neutering in four thirdparty headers �?" the Debian
> `remove_defines` root cause �?" so the flag is now belt-and-suspenders.)

## Build warnings: none (primary build)
The primary gcc `-O3 -Wall -Wextra` build is **0 warnings / 0 errors** (verified
2026-06-27 by a clean `make -j`). The warnings older notes listed here are gone:
the `arcbios.c` `malloc(0)` (`-Walloc-size`) by correction #5, the `core/emul.c:229`
`snprintf` truncation (`-Wformat-truncation`) by correction #4, and the fortify
`memset` `-Wstringop-overflow` over-approximation no longer triggers.

> Other build configs are noisier: a **real `-fsanitize=address,undefined` build**
> (103 MB instrumented binary) emits ~5 pre-existing, non-fatal warnings in
> `debugger.c`/`diskimage.c` (none in the corrected files) �?" these surface only under
> ASan's code transformations. Running the loader regression cases on that ASan build
> shows **0 memory errors** (only a benign exit-time `LeakSanitizer` report). NB: the
> audit tree's `gxemul-asanubsan` (5.96 MB, no `-fsanitize` on its compile lines) was
> not actually instrumented.

## Notes
- The version banner prints `GXemul build` rather than `GXemul 0.7.0` �?" the version
  string isn't embedded in this build (cosmetic; upstream derives it from a
  release index file not present here).
- The applied source fix (`src/core/misc.c` `mystrlcpy`/`mystrlcat`) is compiled
  only on hosts **without** native `strlcpy`; this Gentoo host *has* it, so that
  fix isn't exercised by this particular binary but remains correct for portability.

